50,000 structured vectors of 256 dimensions, 200 queries, recall@10 against exact search

  bits tables  recall@10  vectors scored   build
     8      1        17%           0.9%    0.0s
     8      4        50%           2.7%    0.0s
     8     16        92%          10.6%    0.1s
    12     16        62%           1.5%    0.2s
    12     64        98%           5.5%    0.8s
    16     64        77%           1.1%    1.4s

The best row here found 98% of the true neighbours while scoring 5.5% of the index, using
64 tables — about 51 MB of hash tables for 50,000 vectors, before the vectors themselves.
More bits make buckets smaller, so fewer vectors are scored and true neighbours are split
across buckets; more tables win them back with several independent hashes, and every
table is another copy of the index's bookkeeping. Graph indexes reach higher recall for
less memory on data like this, which is why they replaced hashing in vector databases.
